window.BOOK = [{"chapter": 1, "chapterTitle": "A Small, Expensive Noise", "paragraphs": ["The kettle failed at 06:12, which was how Mara Venn knew it would be a difficult day. There were more serious failures available on the Moon. The air could leave. The electricity could stop. The toilets could develop opinions. But the kettle was the one machine on Relay Station Kestrel that had never pretended to be sophisticated.", "It heated water. Until now.", "Mara held the switch down with a teaspoon. Somewhere behind the galley wall, a relay clicked three times.", "“Don't threaten it,” said Ivo Bell. “It can smell retirement.”", "Ivo had spent thirty-one years maintaining machinery in places where assistance was either expensive or dead. He was repairing a drawer with a strip of foil bearing the words NOT STRUCTURAL.", "The morning sun, barely clearing the crater rim, cast a narrow blade across the galley window. Beyond it lay a landscape that had never once needed a kettle.", "Their duty controller spoke from the ceiling.", "“Unscheduled carrier detected on the heritage band.”", "Mara let go of the switch.", "“How unscheduled?”", "“Forty-three years.”", "Ivo put down the foil.", "“That,” he said, “will complicate the warranty.”"]}, {"chapter": 1, "chapterTitle": "A Small, Expensive Noise", "paragraphs": ["Kestrel occupied a ridge near the lunar south pole, where sunlight was useful, shadows were deep, and every contractor had promised that somebody else's equipment would work at low temperatures.", "Its job was ordinary. It relayed navigation corrections, weather reports from Earth, scientific measurements and personal messages between the surface and the wider network. Much of humanity's growing foothold beyond Earth consisted of waiting for a file to upload.", "Four people lived there. Mara maintained the radios. Ivo maintained everything the inventory system had forgotten. Lin Okafor, station commander, managed power and the fiction that schedules were objective. Dr Saira Malik studied dust and was expected, by strangers, to answer questions about every other part of the universe.", "The duty controller was called Kit. It scheduled tasks, tracked faults and generated sentences from approved templates. It had no consciousness. This had not prevented the crew from blaming it personally for the music in the exercise compartment.", "“What source?” Mara asked.", "“Unresolved. Short packets. Repeating every ninety-six seconds.”", "The speakers made a small, dry crackle.", "Saira appeared in the doorway carrying a sample bag.", "“Have we discovered something?”", "“Possibly,” said Ivo. “But we haven't had tea, so I wouldn't trust us.”"]}, {"chapter": 1, "chapterTitle": "A Small, Expensive Noise", "paragraphs": ["Mara listened first. She had learned that habit from her father, who repaired fishing radios in Fleetwood and believed most faults were trying, in their limited fashion, to explain themselves.", "The packets contained a rising chirp, a pause, then a short burst of digital noise. Not speech. Not the romantic whisper of somebody dying beautifully among the stars.", "She recorded three cycles and compared their arrival times at Kestrel's two receiving dishes. The difference was tiny but consistent.", "“Surface source,” she said. “Roughly northwest.”", "“Reflected?” asked Lin, arriving with her sleeves already rolled up.", "“Maybe. I'd like a second station's bearings.”", "Lin authorised the request. No dramatic argument. Kestrel was an expensive place to have a mystery; checking it properly was cheaper than an accident.", "Kit found a partial match in an archived catalogue: ORISON-4, a robotic seismic lander commissioned in 2046. Its last health report dated from the following year.", "Saira frowned. “That landing site is sixty kilometres from us.”", "“Forty-three years without calling,” Ivo said. “And now it wants technical support.”", "Mara slowed the packet display. Beneath the error flags, a sequence remained stable.", "Not random noise. An identifier, a counter and something that might be a timestamp."]}, {"chapter": 1, "chapterTitle": "A Small, Expensive Noise", "paragraphs": ["Lin wanted the mundane explanations first.", "They checked local transmitters. They shut down the rover beacon for two minutes. Mara compared the signal against known reflections, harmonics and the peculiar interference made by an ageing refrigerator compressor.", "The refrigerator was innocent. For once.", "A second relay returned a bearing that crossed theirs at Orison's old landing ellipse. It was not a precise fix, but it was no longer merely a noise in Kestrel's equipment.", "The archive said Orison had carried a small radioisotope power source, seismic instruments and an experimental low-power mesh receiver. Its main transmitter had failed. A later mission had used its working electronics as a communications testbed, then left it alone.", "“Forty years of standby is plausible,” Saira said. “If the components survived and the power budget stayed very low.”", "“What wakes it?” Lin asked.", "“An event trigger. A timer. A corrupted memory.”", "“A desire to be included,” Ivo suggested.", "Mara looked up the old packet specification. Most of the attachments had vanished into broken corporate archives. One survived as a scanned manual whose pages had been photographed at an angle.", "The future, she thought, would have been considerably easier if the past had labelled its cables."]}, {"chapter": 1, "chapterTitle": "A Small, Expensive Noise", "paragraphs": ["By 08:30 they could read the header.", "The message was not a distress call. Orison had nobody aboard to distress. It was a stored event report, broadcast under an emergency recovery mode after repeated failures to reach its configured relay.", "Its clock claimed the event had occurred seventy-one hours earlier. Its clock could also have been wrong by years.", "The payload held vibration measurements, receiver logs and a system warning: EXTERNAL TIMING CONFLICT.", "Mara felt an unpleasant little recognition.", "Kestrel had recorded a timing discrepancy three days earlier. Twelve milliseconds, dismissed automatically when the next correction arrived. Small enough to hide inside a thousand reasonable explanations. Large enough to matter to machines that navigated by measuring radio travel times.", "Lin pulled up the operations schedule.", "In thirty hours, the cargo tug Peregrine would descend towards South Haven with oxygen, medical supplies and a replacement pump for the ice works. The final approach corridor passed through a section of terrain served by Kestrel's navigation transmissions.", "“Are you connecting those things?” Lin asked.", "“I'm checking whether they're connected.”", "“Good. Keep doing that.”", "Ivo glanced at the untouched kettle.", "“So tea is officially delayed?”", "Mara watched the old lander's next packet arrive.", "“Put it on the fault list.”"]}, {"chapter": 2, "chapterTitle": "Things That Know the Time", "paragraphs": ["The next three hours produced no villain, alien civilisation or useful cup of tea.", "They did produce a spreadsheet.", "Saira aligned Orison's reported event with a small local moonquake recorded by newer instruments. Matching the vibration onset gave them a better time reference than the old lander's internal clock.", "Within the uncertainty of the records, the quake and Kestrel's timing jump had happened together.", "“Movement in the ground?” Lin asked.", "“Enough to shake equipment. Not enough to move our antenna twelve milliseconds' worth of distance.”", "“That sounds like a lot.”", "“About thirty-six hundred kilometres of light travel.”", "Ivo examined the wall. “I'd have noticed the corridor getting that long.”", "Mara plotted the jump against the temperature of the timing rack. An older backup unit had briefly taken over during the quake. Its reference had disagreed with the primary clock, then apparently corrected itself.", "Apparently was doing considerable work.", "The rack had passed its latest scheduled inspection. The record included a green signature from Mara herself.", "She read it twice.", "Then she opened the raw files instead of the report. A pass certificate was evidence that a test had passed, not that the universe had agreed to stop producing faults."]}, {"chapter": 2, "chapterTitle": "Things That Know the Time", "paragraphs": ["Navigation at South Haven used several sources: surface beacons, orbital signals, inertial measurements and optical cameras. None was supposed to become a single point of failure.", "That phrase appeared frequently in documents written after single points of failure had already become expensive.", "Kestrel's broadcasts carried both a clock reference and a correction message. Other equipment compared the information and rejected large disagreements. But small, smooth errors could be more persuasive than obvious failures.", "Mara simulated a twelve-millisecond step. The receiver rejected it immediately.", "She tried a slow drift instead.", "The software accepted more of it than she liked.", "“Still doesn't put a tug into the ground,” Saira said.", "“No. It changes how much trust the tug assigns to the other measurements.”", "“So we warn the pilot.”", "“We warn operations with the evidence. There are forty different software versions out there. I won't invent certainty.”", "Lin wrote the advisory. Suspected local timing fault. Navigation service under review. Independent checks required.", "Earth would hear it in a few seconds. A decision might take longer, because electromagnetic waves travelled faster than organisations.", "The first reply requested the fault reference number.", "Ivo offered to issue one to the kettle so that everyone could feel equally supported."]}, {"chapter": 2, "chapterTitle": "Things That Know the Time", "paragraphs": ["Earth's engineering duty officer was called Emil Rusk. He appeared on the screen with the careful expression of a man who had already been in four meetings before breakfast.", "He did not dismiss them.", "“I need the raw timing data,” he said. “And please don't power-cycle the rack.”", "Mara stopped with her hand over the maintenance menu.", "“I wasn't going to.”", "Ivo looked at her.", "“I was going to consider it professionally.”", "Rusk explained that older racks contained a recovery board designed to preserve timing during network outages. Some units still carried oscillator models whose ageing behaviour had been revised after deployment.", "“Revised how?” Lin asked.", "“I'll get you the bulletin.”", "It arrived eleven minutes later. The bulletin had been sent to the station's previous contractor. The previous contractor's records had been transferred to a successor's archive. That archive had been migrated into a compliance system which contained a reference to the bulletin but not the bulletin itself.", "“Excellent,” Ivo said. “We have achieved distributed responsibility.”", "The fix required a calibration check against an independent reference.", "Kestrel's independent reference had been removed six months earlier during a cost-saving upgrade. The network itself was now supposed to provide the independence.", "Mara stared at the rack.", "The clock was asking its own echo whether it was right."]}, {"chapter": 2, "chapterTitle": "Things That Know the Time", "paragraphs": ["Orison had an explanation, if they could retrieve it.", "Its experimental receiver had measured the old lunar mesh signals using a separate oscillator. That clock was far too old to supply absolute time reliably, but the stored measurements could show a sudden change in the relationship between two transmitters.", "“It doesn't need to know the date,” Mara explained. “A ruler with a faded label can still tell you one thing moved.”", "Unfortunately, emergency mode transmitted only small summaries. The detailed log remained in local storage, accessible through a short-range maintenance link.", "A high-power command from orbit might wake its main interface, but nobody knew whether the old receiver's protection circuits still worked. Sending an untested command could destroy the evidence.", "The safe option was a visit.", "Sixty kilometres was not a casual lunar walk. Their rover could do the round trip with margin, but the route crossed an exposed ridge and had not been inspected since a recent impact survey.", "Lin calculated time, energy and rescue options. Saira reviewed radiation conditions. Ivo checked the rover's pressure seals.", "Mara studied the map and felt the uncomfortable excitement of having a solvable problem in a place where mistakes were permanent.", "“Can we go?” she asked.", "“After breakfast,” Lin said. “Competence works better with calories.”"]}, {"chapter": 2, "chapterTitle": "Things That Know the Time", "paragraphs": ["The cargo tug's operator replied while Mara was eating something officially described as egg.", "Peregrine could delay descent for nineteen hours without disrupting its reserve propellant margin. Beyond that it would need to remain in a higher holding orbit, where unloading would be delayed by several days.", "South Haven had enough oxygen. It did not have much spare pump capacity. The ice works supported eighty-seven people, and its remaining circulation pump had begun sounding like a drawer full of cutlery.", "No immediate catastrophe. A chain of increasingly poor options.", "That was how real emergencies usually introduced themselves.", "Lin requested the nineteen-hour delay provisionally and suspended Kestrel's precision navigation service. The relay would continue ordinary communications. Stopping a questionable clock was safer than letting it testify confidently.", "“Somebody will complain,” Ivo said.", "“Somebody should,” Lin replied. “Then they can read my log.”", "Mara packed two receiver modules, spare connectors, a hand-operated fastening tool and a notebook. The notebook had no radio, no batteries and no way to request a software update.", "Her father had given it to her before her first flight.", "On the inside cover he had written: If it's intermittent, check the thing everyone says can't fail.", "She had found this annoying at nineteen.", "At forty-two it was practically a religion."]}, {"chapter": 3, "chapterTitle": "The Scenic Route", "paragraphs": ["The rover was called Ada, after a computing pioneer. Ivo called it The Van, after a van.", "It carried Mara and Ivo in a small pressurised cabin, with emergency suits worn open at the neck and helmets secured within reach. Every kilogram had a reason for being there. A few had several conflicting reasons.", "Lin watched from the airlock window as they rolled away.", "“Route marks uploaded. Turn back if your reserve falls below the agreed limit.”", "“Understood.”", "“That was for both of you.”", "Ivo adjusted his seat. “My recklessness is mostly theoretical these days.”", "Kestrel shrank behind them. The terrain alternated between pale ridges and black hollows. Without air to soften distances, a hill could look close enough to touch while being twenty minutes away.", "Mara checked the independent inertial unit they had borrowed from the geology kit. It would drift, but slowly enough for the route checks they needed.", "For a while neither spoke.", "Then Ivo opened the food locker.", "“You packed the emergency biscuits.”", "“Yes.”", "“The ones labelled twelve servings?”", "“Yes.”", "“That is a deeply optimistic assessment of twelve emergencies.”", "He ate one anyway, carefully, so the crumbs could not reach the cabin filters."]}, {"chapter": 3, "chapterTitle": "The Scenic Route", "paragraphs": ["At the first ridge, the planned route ended in fresh debris.", "A small impact had thrown stones across the traverse. The rover's automatic pathfinder suggested a neat arc through a darker patch downslope.", "Ivo stopped.", "“Why?” Mara asked.", "“Too neat.”", "They compared stereo images. The dark patch was not a smooth floor but a slope in shadow. The system's confidence score had risen because the texture was simple, which was not the same as the ground being safe.", "“Another machine that enjoys certainty,” Mara said.", "“Machines are excellent. Confidence is usually an accessory fitted by people.”", "They sent the images to Kestrel. Saira found an older overhead pass with enough detail to identify a narrow alternative above the debris.", "The detour cost forty minutes and some energy, all within their margin. Lin revised their latest departure time from Orison.", "Mara disliked losing the minutes. She disliked that she disliked it. That was how pressure worked: it made a shortcut feel like a fact.", "She logged the detour, then marked the rejected route explicitly as unsafe.", "Some future driver might be tired, worried and in a hurry. They deserved better than a confident green line."]}, {"chapter": 3, "chapterTitle": "The Scenic Route", "paragraphs": ["Halfway to Orison, South Haven called.", "The voice belonged to its chief operator, Tomas Vale, who had once worked with Mara on a coastal emergency network before deciding that terrestrial weather lacked variety.", "“I hear you've switched off the accurate time.”", "“Only the suspiciously accurate time.”", "“Our pump is definitely inaccurate. It keeps thinking it's a percussion instrument.”", "“Are you all right?”", "A pause.", "“We've reduced production. Reserves are good. The medical cargo's more urgent than people realise, but we've got options.”", "He did not tell her to hurry. She appreciated that more than she could say.", "He asked whether the lander might be recoverable.", "“For the museum?”", "“For spare connectors. Our museum is less ambitious.”", "After the call, Ivo looked through the side window.", "“You know everyone eventually, out here.”", "“That good or bad?”", "“Yes.”", "Mara thought of the eighty-seven people waiting for a pump, and the tug crew waiting for a safe clearance. They were not numbers in an optimisation problem. They were people whose jokes she recognised.", "She checked the reserve display again.", "Still safe.", "Still no reason to drive faster."]}, {"chapter": 3, "chapterTitle": "The Scenic Route", "paragraphs": ["Orison appeared over a low rise like a tin shed that had lost an argument with a quarry.", "One landing leg had settled deeper than the others. Its main antenna pointed into a ridge. A dust-darkened instrument boom lay crooked beside a circular thermal shield.", "Nothing glowed. Nothing moved.", "Mara had expected, absurdly, that a machine calling across forty-three years might look expectant.", "They parked outside the debris field and spent ten minutes checking the scene before opening the cabin. The radioisotope source remained shielded. Its old safety marker was almost unreadable, but the detector confirmed no reason to approach that compartment.", "They only needed the electronics bay on the opposite side.", "Outside, sound came from their suits: breathing, fans, the soft transmission noise of two people trying not to interrupt each other.", "Ivo planted a marker at the rover and attached a safety line for the sloping approach. Lunar gravity reduced their weight, not their inertia. A stumble could still carry someone a long way.", "Mara knelt beside the maintenance port.", "The cover fastener resisted her tool.", "“Forty-three years,” she muttered.", "“Don't rush it,” Ivo said. “It's had a quiet life.”"]}, {"chapter": 3, "chapterTitle": "The Scenic Route", "paragraphs": ["The port cover came free without drama, which Mara considered an insult to several years of training but accepted gratefully.", "Her connector did not fit.", "She looked at it. Then at the port. Then, very carefully, at the manual.", "The photographed page showed the flight model. Orison's serial number corresponded to an earlier engineering revision.", "“I brought the wrong adaptor.”", "Ivo did not say anything for a few seconds.", "“That's unusually generous of you,” she said.", "“I was choosing between several remarks.”", "They could fabricate an electrical bridge, but not by guessing the pin assignments. A reversed supply line could end the investigation permanently.", "Mara sent a close image of the port to Kestrel and asked Lin to search the old test records.", "While they waited, Ivo examined a torn insulating blanket. A small stamped plate beneath it carried a manufacturer's mark.", "“I know this company.”", "“Still exists?”", "“No. But their engineers were fond of putting the useful information on the actual machine.”", "He brushed loose dust away with the approved tool, slowly enough not to drive it into the connector.", "Beneath the port was a tiny engraved diagram.", "Mara read it twice.", "Then she photographed it for the future."]}, {"chapter": 4, "chapterTitle": "The Machine in the Cold", "paragraphs": ["They made the bridge inside the rover, where fingers and loose components were more manageable.", "Ivo fitted spring contacts into a spare diagnostic clamp. Mara checked each conductor against the engraving, then checked it again without referring to her first notes.", "Lin found the engineering revision in an archive held by a university in Nairobi. The pin assignments matched.", "Only then did they connect.", "Orison answered at a data rate that would have embarrassed a doorbell.", "“It's alive,” Ivo said.", "“It's powered.”", "“Let me have something.”", "The status page showed a declining power budget, cold-related memory errors and a message queue that had never cleared. The old lander had been listening for decades. When the quake disturbed its receive chain, a hardware watchdog had restarted the logging unit and enabled the emergency transmitter.", "Its clock had lost calendar accuracy, but its event sequence remained consistent.", "Mara requested the detailed log. Estimated transfer time: seventy-eight minutes.", "Their latest safe departure allowed sixty-four.", "She selected only the channels surrounding the event.", "Estimated transfer time: thirty-nine minutes.", "“There,” said Ivo. “Half the information, twice the excitement.”", "They watched the first blocks arrive, each verified before it was written to local storage."]}, {"chapter": 4, "chapterTitle": "The Machine in the Cold", "paragraphs": ["The log showed two things.", "First, Kestrel's old backup timing board had transmitted a step change during the quake.", "Second, a different source had begun repeating an old correction sequence six minutes later.", "The source identifier belonged to Lantern, a retired mesh repeater fourteen kilometres beyond Orison.", "Mara pulled up the network map. Lantern was marked decommissioned.", "“Meaning?” Ivo asked.", "“Removed from service.”", "“Not necessarily switched off.”", "“No.”", "Lantern had once extended coverage around the ridge. Its local solar panels and battery maintained a low-power emergency mode after retirement. The old arrangement had seemed harmless: an unused repeater transmitting only when it detected a recognised network failure.", "Except it still recognised Kestrel's heritage channel.", "A timing step had looked like the failure condition it was built to address. Lantern had responded with the last valid correction sequence it held.", "That sequence was forty years out of date.", "“Why isn't the modern network rejecting it?” Ivo asked.", "“It is. Mostly. But Kestrel's recovery board still trusts its identifier.”", "The evidence was not complete. A repeated pattern was not yet a complete failure mechanism.", "Mara requested the remaining source records.", "Outside, the shadows reached slowly across Orison's old feet."]}, {"chapter": 4, "chapterTitle": "The Machine in the Cold", "paragraphs": ["Rusk called while the last data blocks were arriving.", "“We reproduced the first step,” he said. “A loose reference connection in the recovery board can trigger holdover. The old firmware then accepts heritage corrections if the primary link is uncertain.”", "“And Lantern gives it one.”", "“Yes. But we need your logs to establish the cycle.”", "Mara sent a compressed sample through the rover's dish. Earth remained conveniently near in radio terms and inconveniently far in practical ones.", "Rusk examined it with another engineer off-screen.", "“That's a recovery loop,” he said at last. “Your backup accepts the repeater. The network correction pulls it back. It enters holdover again. Once a thermal transition worsens the reference connection, the loop can repeat.”", "“Can we isolate the board?”", "“Yes, physically. Disabling it in software doesn't isolate the reference path on that revision.”", "Ivo closed his eyes briefly.", "He had argued against that board during the last refit. Mara remembered his objection as an old technician's suspicion of anything described as seamless.", "She wished she had remembered it differently.", "“Send us the isolation procedure,” she said.", "Ivo opened his eyes.", "“Good,” he said. “A fault we can actually put a spanner near.”"]}, {"chapter": 4, "chapterTitle": "The Machine in the Cold", "paragraphs": ["A warning sounded in Mara's headset.", "Her suit's cooling loop pressure had fallen below its expected range.", "Not a major loss. Not yet an emergency. Enough to stop work.", "She told Ivo, checked the reading against the secondary sensor and began the return to the rover. She wanted to collect the diagnostic clamp first. It was only a few metres away.", "Ivo heard the hesitation.", "“Leave it.”", "“It took an hour to make.”", "“So did your lunch. Priorities.”", "Inside the rover they sealed the hatch, repressurised and followed the suit procedure. A coupling had accumulated abrasive dust. The redundant path maintained cooling, but continuing the excursion would have spent a protection layer they might need later.", "Mara felt foolish. She also felt alive, which was a useful counterargument.", "Through the window, their clamp remained attached to Orison like a visiting insect.", "Ivo handed her a drink pouch.", "“Didn't lose the data,” he said.", "“Nearly made a bad decision.”", "“Nearly is where most good decisions live. People forget the first half.”", "She drank, then reported the fault honestly to Lin.", "There was a pause.", "“Thank you for turning back,” Lin said.", "Not what Mara had expected.", "Exactly what she needed."]}, {"chapter": 4, "chapterTitle": "The Machine in the Cold", "paragraphs": ["They departed Orison with twelve minutes of schedule margin and less certainty than they had hoped, but enough evidence to act.", "Mara looked back once. The lander had not saved anyone deliberately. It had recorded measurements, detected a failure condition and transmitted what it could.", "There was something comforting in that.", "No guardian intelligence. No secret purpose. Just a machine that still performed one small job after the people who specified it had grown old.", "“Should we say thank you?” Ivo asked.", "“I logged the serial number.”", "“Engineers. Such poets.”", "They left Lantern alone. Fourteen kilometres farther meant twenty-eight added to the return, terrain they had not surveyed and a breach of their agreed reserve.", "A dramatic expedition to silence the transmitter might have made an excellent story. It would have made a poor decision.", "Instead, they would remove its influence at Kestrel.", "Lin approved the plan. Rusk's team began preparing a test with an independent reference transmitted from an orbital platform. The signal would have its own uncertainties, but different ones.", "Mara watched the rover's tracks vanish behind a rise.", "For the first time that day she thought the problem might have an edge they could hold.", "Then Ada's traction alarm sounded."]}, {"chapter": 5, "chapterTitle": "Getting Home Is a Feature", "paragraphs": ["The left rear wheel had entered a pocket of loose material beside a buried rock. It was turning, slowly and uselessly.", "Ivo stopped all drive commands.", "“Before you ask,” he said, “more power is the traditional way to make this worse.”", "Mara had not asked. She had thought it very loudly.", "They checked the slope. The rover remained stable. No pressure loss. No battery fault. The simple facts were worth saying aloud.", "The recovery kit included a traction mat and a folding excavation blade. Using it would require another excursion.", "Mara's suit could not be trusted for routine work until serviced.", "“I go out,” Ivo said. “You operate the winch from here.”", "They rehearsed the sequence, checked his suit, then checked the route between the hatch and the wheel. He moved slowly outside, his safety line making an awkward bright curve against the grey surface.", "Mara watched the oxygen and battery margins.", "They still had enough.", "Enough had become a more interesting word than plenty.", "Ivo cleared the shallow lip in front of the wheel. He set the mat, returned to a safe position and gave the signal.", "Mara applied the smallest useful torque.", "The rover moved six centimetres.", "They stopped and checked again."]}, {"chapter": 5, "chapterTitle": "Getting Home Is a Feature", "paragraphs": ["It took twenty-three minutes to recover six metres of ground.", "Nobody would have put that on a promotional film.", "Ivo stowed the tools, inspected the wheel and returned through the hatch with a dust coating that made him look as though he had been breaded.", "“The Moon,” he announced, “has poor parking.”", "Their departure margin had gone. Their safety margin had not. Lin helped them revise the return route, avoiding the uncertain shortcut suggested by the automatic planner.", "Mara expected anger from herself, but what came was concentration. They could not win the lost time back by wanting it.", "She told Rusk that the isolation procedure would begin later than planned.", "He told her that Peregrine had moved into its agreed hold.", "The tug's commander, Nia Serrat, added a short voice message.", "“We've got fuel for the plan. Follow yours.”", "Mara played it twice.", "Then she shut down the nonessential display, dimmed the cabin lighting and checked the route marks against the inertial unit.", "Ivo watched her.", "“Saving watts?”", "“And reducing distractions.”", "“Would you like me off?”", "“Do you have a quiet mode?”", "“Not in this revision.”"]}, {"chapter": 5, "chapterTitle": "Getting Home Is a Feature", "paragraphs": ["On the drive home, Mara finally read the original refit discussion.", "Her own note sat beneath Ivo's objection: Heritage recovery chain acceptable under current monitoring assumptions.", "It was a sensible sentence. That was the worst part.", "Monitoring assumed the network references were independent. The upgrade had reduced that independence. The station's staffing had changed. A decommissioned repeater had stayed powered. No single choice looked like the beginning of a crisis.", "Together they made a clock that could be wrong while its checks agreed.", "“I should have pushed harder,” she said.", "Ivo looked over.", "“At the refit.”", "“So should I.”", "“You did.”", "“I grumbled. That isn't a test plan.”", "She wanted absolution and disliked discovering it. Instead he gave her the practical shape of responsibility.", "“We write down what we missed,” he said. “We change the test. We don't turn the report into a competition for who feels worst.”", "Outside, a blade of sun lit the ridge ahead.", "Mara opened a new incident note and began with her own assumption.", "Not because it was the only cause.", "Because she knew its author, and could make sure it did not disappear into the language of somebody else's process."]}, {"chapter": 5, "chapterTitle": "Getting Home Is a Feature", "paragraphs": ["Kestrel's lights became visible before its walls did.", "Lin met them in the dust-control compartment. Saira took Ivo's samples and inspected Mara's suit coupling. Nobody gave a speech.", "There were tasks to do.", "The timing rack stood in a service bay with the proportions and warmth of a domestic airing cupboard. Unlike a domestic airing cupboard, it contained several systems upon which landing spacecraft relied.", "Mara and Ivo read Rusk's procedure aloud, step by step. Lin checked power isolation. Saira kept the record.", "The recovery board came free with a small resistance, then lay in Mara's hands.", "At one connector a retaining clip had cracked. Repeated thermal cycles and a design that put mechanical strain on the reference lead had done most of the damage. The quake had probably supplied the final movement.", "“There's our intermittent fault,” Mara said.", "Ivo leaned close, not touching.", "“Thing everyone says can't fail?”", "She thought of the notebook.", "“Thing nobody looked at twice.”", "The navigation output remained disabled. They had removed a known fault, but removal was not proof of recovery.", "Mara inserted a passive terminator into the exposed reference path and began the first independent test."]}, {"chapter": 5, "chapterTitle": "Getting Home Is a Feature", "paragraphs": ["The first test failed.", "For twelve glorious seconds, all three displays agreed. Then the orbital reference showed Kestrel's primary output slipping.", "Not the same error. A different one, smaller and regular.", "Mara sat back.", "“Tell me that's a measurement problem.”", "Saira checked the orbital path delay calculation.", "“It could be. I won't tell you it is.”", "They repeated the test using a second satellite pass. The discrepancy remained.", "The removed board had not only introduced bad corrections. It had also been masking ageing in the primary oscillator by continuously steering it. Without the recovery path, the old clock's true drift became visible.", "“We need a replacement reference,” Mara said.", "Their spare oscillator was in the cargo locker.", "It was also in the cargo manifest for Peregrine.", "Ivo looked towards the ceiling as if there might be a manager above it.", "“Of course it is.”", "Lin asked whether Kestrel could remain out of the navigation service.", "For most traffic, yes. For Peregrine's scheduled southward approach, the geometry left a coverage gap that mattered if dust obscured the final optical check.", "They could wait several days for a different approach window.", "South Haven's pump had not promised to wait with them."]}, {"chapter": 6, "chapterTitle": "A Better Kind of Wrong", "paragraphs": ["At 22:10, the crew held a meeting around a table too small for the number of bad options.", "Lin listed them without decoration. Wait for another approach window. Move the tug's cargo to a smaller lander. Establish a temporary reference. Accept a reduced navigation service and let the tug's commander decide whether to attempt descent.", "“No one says accept the risk as though the risk were a parcel,” she added. “We describe it. Nia decides for her vehicle. South Haven decides for its operations.”", "The smaller lander was already supporting a medical evacuation on the far side. Recalling it would move urgency between people rather than solve anything.", "Waiting remained safe for Peregrine but increasingly costly for South Haven.", "Mara considered the portable geology receiver. It contained a stable oscillator, but stability alone was not enough. They would have to determine its offset, characterise its drift and connect it to a transmitter without adding unknown delays.", "“Can it work?” Lin asked.", "“In principle.”", "Ivo rubbed his eyes.", "“A phrase with an excellent casualty record.”", "Mara nodded.", "“So we test the principle before we put anyone on top of it.”", "Lin assigned them four hours, then a mandatory rest period."]}, {"chapter": 6, "chapterTitle": "A Better Kind of Wrong", "paragraphs": ["The geology receiver had been designed to measure small differences in arrival time between seismic stations. Its oscillator was good. Its connectors were not compatible with anything Mara owned, a consistent theme in her professional life.", "Saira found the calibration records.", "“I knew you'd ask,” she said. “My equipment isn't magic just because you don't understand why I bought it.”", "Mara smiled despite herself.", "They built a reference chain on the bench. Orbital signals established time. The geology unit held it through brief interruptions. A spare communications modem generated a test beacon.", "The first measurements looked promising.", "Then Ivo put his palm near the adaptor lead. The displayed timing shifted.", "He took his hand away. It shifted back.", "“Congratulations,” he said. “We've invented a clock powered by whether I'm hovering.”", "A poorly shielded connection was picking up interference from the modem. They replaced the temporary lead with a properly terminated cable and enclosed the interface in a grounded case.", "The effect vanished.", "Mara added the test to the log.", "A functioning prototype was not a product. A product was a prototype that had survived people asking unpleasant questions about it.", "Outside, the narrow sunlight moved off their preferred power panels. Kestrel began drawing more heavily from storage."]}, {"chapter": 6, "chapterTitle": "A Better Kind of Wrong", "paragraphs": ["Their emergency beacon needed an antenna with a clear view of the southern approach.", "Kestrel's main dishes were occupied with communications traffic and the independent timing checks. A spare low-gain antenna could cover the landing corridor, but the station wall blocked part of its view from the service platform.", "The old survey mast on the ridge had the right geometry.", "It was six hundred metres away.", "Mara inspected the exterior camera. Between the station and the mast lay a shallow hollow containing a permanently shadowed strip. It was a familiar maintenance route, marked and surveyed, but still an excursion.", "Her suit remained out of service. Ivo's fatigue score was approaching the station limit.", "“I can do it,” Saira said.", "“You're not a radio engineer.”", "“No. I'm a field scientist qualified for this route, trained on the mast, and currently less likely than either of you to put a glove on backwards.”", "Lin checked her records, then nodded.", "“I go with you. Mara handles the installation checks from inside.”", "Mara started to object, then heard what she would be objecting to: other competent people being competent without her.", "She took out the antenna's assembly guide.", "“Right. Let's make these instructions usable with cold fingers.”"]}, {"chapter": 6, "chapterTitle": "A Better Kind of Wrong", "paragraphs": ["Before they rested, Mara called her father.", "She had planned a brief message. She did not want to worry him. But her tired face on his kitchen screen had already done most of that work.", "“Long day?” he asked.", "“Couple of clock faults.”", "“Ah. Time itself. Always buying the cheapest components.”", "She laughed, then unexpectedly found her eyes wet.", "He waited.", "“I signed off a system that wasn't independent enough,” she said.", "“Have you made it safe?”", "“We've stopped the bad service. We're building another.”", "“Then do that. Afterwards you can be sensible about what went wrong.”", "Through his window she could see rain, ordinary Earth rain, running down glass. A car went past with music playing. The abundance of air suddenly seemed ridiculous.", "“Do you ever think I should have stayed?” she asked.", "He looked at her for a moment.", "“I think you should get some sleep.”", "“That wasn't the question.”", "“It was the useful answer.”", "After the call she set a ninety-minute alarm and lay down.", "For once, she did not listen for faults in the fans.", "She let somebody else's watch be somebody else's watch."]}, {"chapter": 6, "chapterTitle": "A Better Kind of Wrong", "paragraphs": ["While Mara slept, Lin received a message from the regional operations manager.", "Could Kestrel restore limited service while the replacement beacon was evaluated? The outage was creating measurable disruption.", "Lin replied with the current uncertainty bounds and a clear no.", "Then she attached the evidence, the alternatives and the next decision time.", "She did not accuse the manager of cowardice or indifference. He was responsible for a busy network and was asking a reasonable question. A reasonable question could still have the answer no.", "His next message thanked her and confirmed support.", "Lin let out a breath she had not realised she was holding.", "In the workshop Saira checked the geology receiver's battery pack, then labelled every adaptor with its purpose. Ivo stood by the door, finishing a drink before his rest period.", "“You think it'll work?” he asked.", "“I think we'll find out.”", "“Terrible attitude for a scientist.”", "She looked up.", "“Very popular in the literature.”", "Kit announced an unassigned fault.", "Lin opened it.", "GALLEY WATER HEATER: LOW PRIORITY.", "For the first time all day she nearly smiled.", "“Remain low priority,” she told it.", "“Instruction recorded,” said Kit.", "It was not a joke. That helped."]}, {"chapter": 7, "chapterTitle": "The Long Cable", "paragraphs": ["Mara woke to the smell of hot food and the encouraging absence of alarms.", "Ivo had made lentil stew. The recipe had been developed for long-duration missions, which appeared to mean that nobody could escape it.", "“It tastes better than it looks,” he said.", "“A low technical threshold.”", "“It has won awards.”", "“Which ones?”", "“None. But you looked hopeful for a moment.”", "They ate. They reviewed the reference tests. The oscillator had remained within their provisional limit through two independent orbital comparisons. Rusk's team checked the calculations and requested one change: report the uncertainty explicitly in every beacon packet, rather than using Kestrel's normal service rating.", "The new beacon would be less precise than the station's service in its healthy state. It would also be honest about what it knew.", "Nia's navigation officer simulated the approach with the wider error bounds. Acceptable, provided the signal stayed stable and optical checks remained available above the final abort point.", "No promise to land. Permission to keep preparing.", "Mara preferred it that way.", "At the airlock, Lin and Saira checked each other's seals.", "Ivo watched the checklist display.", "Nobody hurried them."]}, {"chapter": 7, "chapterTitle": "The Long Cable", "paragraphs": ["The survey mast had stood on the ridge for eleven years. Its camera had failed after three, but its foundation and protected cable duct remained sound.", "Lin and Saira carried the folded antenna between them. It was light enough to lift easily and awkward enough to make easy lifting a dangerous temptation.", "Mara followed on the exterior cameras.", "“Mind the lower brace,” she said.", "“We can see it,” Saira replied.", "A pause.", "“Sorry.”", "“Accepted. You can tell me when I'm about to do something interesting.”", "They secured the antenna to the mount, checked its orientation against two surveyed markers and attached the feed line.", "The first signal reached the test receiver weakly.", "Too weakly.", "Mara checked power at the modem. Correct. Corrected cable losses. Still wrong.", "Ivo studied the old duct diagram.", "“There are two cable runs.”", "“One is spare.”", "“According to the drawing.”", "They asked Saira to inspect the termination labels. Both had faded to the same uniform grey.", "Mara closed her eyes briefly.", "“Please don't tell me we've spent the day defeating a faulty clock only to be stopped by a label.”", "Ivo picked up a continuity tester.", "“Civilisation has rested on thinner things.”"]}, {"chapter": 7, "chapterTitle": "The Long Cable", "paragraphs": ["The cable they had connected was intact. It also ran to an old lightning test enclosure beneath the service platform, not to the radio bay.", "There was no weather on the Moon, but there had been an Earth-based qualification programme and a contractor who liked reusing terminology.", "The second cable was the correct one. It had a damaged shield.", "Mara felt the plan narrow.", "They could move a timing unit outside near the antenna and use the cable only for data, but that would require thermal protection they had not tested. Or they could lay a new feed line across the route.", "They had a reel of suitable cable. It was longer than needed, and its propagation delay would have to be measured after installation.", "Lin checked the excursion margins.", "“Enough time for one careful run.”", "Ivo readied the reel at the airlock. Saira returned to collect its end while Lin inspected the marked route for places where the cable could snag or cross equipment access.", "Mara wrote a new test sequence, deleting the old expected values rather than altering them from memory.", "The easy version of the plan was gone.", "The possible version remained.", "“It's just cable,” she said.", "“Most of civilisation,” Ivo replied, “is just cable.”"]}, {"chapter": 7, "chapterTitle": "The Long Cable", "paragraphs": ["The new line unrolled behind Saira in a dark, deliberate curve.", "She secured it at intervals with existing route clips, leaving slack where thermal contraction might otherwise pull at the connectors. Lin protected the crossing at the rover access path with a rigid cover from the maintenance stores.", "Mara watched the signal trace appear.", "Strong. Clean.", "She said neither word aloud until she had checked the power reading and the spectrum.", "Then she said both.", "“Lovely,” Saira replied. “Does lovely mean we can come indoors?”", "“After the mechanical check.”", "“I feared as much.”", "They inspected the mount, connectors and restraints. Ivo photographed the installation through the station's higher-resolution exterior camera so future maintenance would not depend on somebody remembering what a grey cable had looked like.", "The antenna made a modest rectangle against the black sky.", "No one would have mistaken it for a machine that could help eighty-seven people receive a pump. It looked like a particularly expensive clothes airer.", "Mara thought that was appropriate.", "Useful things rarely looked as important as they were.", "When Lin and Saira returned, she met them at the inner hatch with warmed towels from the medical cupboard.", "It was the best welcome she could arrange without a kettle."]}, {"chapter": 7, "chapterTitle": "The Long Cable", "paragraphs": ["Calibration occupied the next two hours.", "They measured the new cable delay, then measured it again using a different method. They checked the receiver's offset against two orbital sources. They transmitted test packets at reduced power and asked independent stations to report what they received.", "Mara expected the first real difficulty to be technical.", "Instead, the regional monitoring software marked the temporary beacon as unauthorised and tried to exclude it from the operational map.", "“Accurate,” Ivo said. “We haven't asked it nicely enough.”", "Lin obtained a temporary identifier and a written service notice. Nia's crew loaded the beacon parameters through their controlled update process. Nobody renamed it Kestrel and hoped the tug would not notice.", "At the end of the tests, the uncertainty budget stayed within the limit agreed for the approach. The number was less impressive than the old green status light.", "It was considerably more useful.", "Mara signed the results, with Saira as an independent reviewer.", "Rusk's team confirmed them from Earth.", "Peregrine's commander accepted the beacon as an additional navigation source.", "Then Nia asked a simple question.", "“What happens if you lose power during descent?”", "Mara looked towards the station battery display.", "Nobody answered immediately."]}, {"chapter": 8, "chapterTitle": "Power Has to Come from Somewhere", "paragraphs": ["The temporary system consumed less power than the main navigation rack. That was the good part.", "It drew from a workshop circuit whose emergency supply had never been rated for a safety-related service. That was the other part.", "The station batteries had adequate energy for the descent window, but a circuit fault could disconnect the modem without affecting the main control room. A healthy battery symbol would not prove the transmitter still had power.", "“I should have checked that first,” Mara said.", "“Check it now,” Lin replied.", "They found a medical transport battery with a suitable approved converter. It was intended to keep equipment alive during transfers between habitats. It had an independent status output, enough capacity and a maintenance record untroubled by missing bulletins.", "Connecting it safely took another forty minutes and three pages of documentation.", "Ivo attached the final cable, then placed a plain-language label above it.", "TEMPORARY NAVIGATION BEACON. DO NOT UNPLUG TO CHARGE A TABLET.", "“Too informal?” he asked.", "Lin read it.", "“Clear.”", "They tested the change by removing workshop power while transmitting to a test receiver.", "The beacon remained stable.", "Mara watched the trace without blinking.", "For once the machine did exactly what the paperwork said it would."]}, {"chapter": 8, "chapterTitle": "Power Has to Come from Somewhere", "paragraphs": ["South Haven sent a short operational update.", "The pump had survived another reduced-output cycle. The medical team had rearranged supplies. Everyone was managing.", "At the bottom of the message Tomas had added a photograph: three people in the workshop holding a sign that read PLEASE BRING THE QUIET PUMP.", "Ivo laughed hard enough to cough.", "Mara saved the image, then opened the descent timeline.", "Peregrine would lower its orbit, verify the approach corridor and begin descent only after all checks agreed. Above a defined altitude, any navigation conflict would trigger an abort back to a safe trajectory. Below it, the vehicle relied increasingly on local terrain sensing.", "The temporary beacon reduced uncertainty during the transition. It did not fly the ship.", "Mara found that distinction calming.", "Her job had an edge. She could measure it, test it and tell somebody else what it could do.", "She could not guarantee the Moon.", "Lin assigned stations. Saira monitored independent comparisons. Ivo watched power and thermal status. Mara handled the beacon and its logs. Lin coordinated with the tug and surface operations.", "Kit offered to generate a mission confidence summary.", "“No, thank you,” Lin said.", "It sounded almost tender."]}, {"chapter": 8, "chapterTitle": "Power Has to Come from Somewhere", "paragraphs": ["Thirty minutes before the descent review, the beacon's uncertainty estimate began to widen.", "Not a jump. A slow, smooth trend.", "Mara checked the oscillator temperature. The geology receiver's internal heater had entered a different control cycle. On a laboratory bench the effect would have been an interesting note. In their current application it consumed margin.", "“Still inside the limit,” Saira said.", "“For how long?”", "They used the calibration model to project the drift, then compared the projection against the next five minutes of measurements. Close, but not close enough to relax.", "Ivo inspected the receiver enclosure. The workshop ventilation had changed when the station shifted its thermal load.", "“We can stabilise the room,” he said. “We don't touch the unit.”", "Lin checked the implications for habitation. They could reduce galley heating and postpone a battery recharge. Nobody would become unsafe. Several people would become mildly uncomfortable.", "“Proceed.”", "The temperature trend flattened slowly.", "Mara wanted to reach into the plot and pull it flat herself.", "Instead she reported the excursion and revised the uncertainty figure sent to Peregrine.", "Nia's reply came promptly.", "“Received. Stay with the measurements.”", "Mara took her hands off the controls.", "That was harder than it sounded."]}, {"chapter": 8, "chapterTitle": "Power Has to Come from Somewhere", "paragraphs": ["At the descent review, one member of the tug's team recommended waiting for the next orbit.", "Mara's stomach tightened.", "The delay would push the final approach closer to another period of poor illumination. It might still be safer. The person making the recommendation had the right to be cautious without becoming the villain in Kestrel's story.", "Nia asked for a comparison of the two windows.", "The team reviewed fuel, terrain lighting, optical sensor performance, beacon uncertainty and the surface demand. Their voices remained maddeningly calm.", "Mara sat on her impulse to explain again how hard they had worked.", "Hard work was not a navigation measurement.", "The tug's officer withdrew the recommendation after the updated optical assessment. The present window offered the better abort options and a stronger independent check during the beacon transition.", "Nia made the decision.", "“Proceed to deorbit preparation. Final commit remains conditional.”", "Mara let out a breath.", "Ivo passed her a drink.", "“No medals yet.”", "“Wasn't expecting one.”", "“Good. We've only got adhesive labels.”", "On the main display, Peregrine's predicted track curved towards the southern horizon.", "It was a line on a screen.", "Somewhere above it, four people were tightening their harnesses."]}, {"chapter": 8, "chapterTitle": "Power Has to Come from Somewhere", "paragraphs": ["The deorbit burn completed within tolerance.", "Peregrine crossed behind a ridge from Kestrel's viewpoint, still linked through an orbital relay. Its telemetry showed the steady small changes of a vehicle doing what it was designed to do.", "Mara monitored the beacon's output and compared it with the tug's received timing residuals. The numbers stayed boring.", "She began to love them.", "Then Saira lifted one hand.", "“Independent check lost.”", "An orbital platform had dropped their monitoring channel during a scheduled antenna handover. The handover had been in the plan. The loss was longer than expected.", "The temporary oscillator could hold its reference through an interruption, but the uncertainty estimate would grow until the independent check returned.", "Mara notified Nia immediately.", "“Holdover active. Current estimate within agreed bounds. Updating every ten seconds.”", "“Received.”", "They had calculated the maximum acceptable interval. Ninety seconds.", "At forty, Ivo checked power. At fifty, Saira tried the secondary receiver. At sixty, Lin asked the orbital controller for status.", "No one said that it would be fine.", "At seventy-three seconds the independent check returned.", "The measured offset agreed with the prediction.", "Mara reported it before she allowed herself to breathe properly.", "Ivo looked at the wall clock.", "“Longest minute I've had all week.”", "It had been seventy-three seconds. Nobody corrected him."]}, {"chapter": 9, "chapterTitle": "The Part Where Nothing Happens", "paragraphs": ["Peregrine acquired the temporary beacon on approach.", "Its navigation system compared the signal with inertial estimates and optical landmarks. The new source received a modest weighting consistent with its declared uncertainty.", "It was not glamorous. It was exactly right.", "Mara watched the received residual settle.", "“Beacon healthy,” she said.", "Saira confirmed the independent time comparison. Ivo reported stable power. Lin relayed the summaries, resisting the temptation to narrate every moving number.", "The tug began its powered descent.", "For a while, nothing happened.", "Mara had spent most of her career trying to achieve this state. She was disappointed to discover how frightening success could feel when it mattered enough.", "A cupboard door ticked as the workshop cooled. Someone's stomach growled. The ventilation made its usual uneven whistle above the service bay.", "“After this,” Ivo said quietly, “I am fixing that fan.”", "Mara almost laughed.", "“Put it on the list.”", "“It is on the list. The list is becoming a historical document.”", "On the display, Peregrine passed its next decision gate.", "Nia reported agreement between navigation sources.", "“Continuing descent.”", "Mara wrote the time in her father's notebook, because the electronic log already had it and she needed something useful for her hands to do."]}, {"chapter": 9, "chapterTitle": "The Part Where Nothing Happens", "paragraphs": ["The optical system lost one landmark in deep shadow.", "A yellow indicator appeared, accompanied by an advisory that looked, to Mara, much more serious than the word advisory deserved.", "The other landmarks remained valid. Radar altitude agreed with the terrain model. The beacon remained stable.", "Peregrine's navigation officer called the check aloud.", "“Single optical feature rejected. Solution within bounds.”", "Mara looked away from the video feed and back at her own instruments. She could do nothing about a boulder in a spacecraft camera. She could make sure the radio did not add another uncertainty.", "“Output nominal.”", "The signal trace trembled slightly, then settled. Ordinary receiver noise. She knew its statistics and still disliked it personally.", "Saira put a fresh comparison plot beside hers.", "Both agreed.", "The rejected landmark fell behind the tug. A new one entered view, brighter and cleanly resolved.", "The yellow indicator cleared.", "Ivo's shoulders lowered by a fraction.", "“Moon's still in the expected place,” he said.", "“Within tolerance,” Saira replied.", "They did not laugh loudly. It was not a room for that yet.", "But Mara felt the remark loosen something essential.", "They were frightened people doing careful work.", "The work did not require them to stop being people."]}, {"chapter": 9, "chapterTitle": "The Part Where Nothing Happens", "paragraphs": ["At the final abort gate, Nia paused for the required checks.", "Peregrine had fuel margin. The landing zone was clear. Terrain sensing had a strong local solution. Navigation sources remained consistent.", "“Commit,” she said.", "From this point the beacon became less important. The vehicle could see and measure the ground beneath it. Kestrel had helped carry it across the uncertain part.", "Mara knew that intellectually. Her hands did not appear to have received the notice.", "The video showed grey terrain sliding beneath the tug. Small craters. Tracks from a survey rover. The rectangular shadow of the landing pad's marker equipment.", "Then the plume struck loose dust.", "The image blurred.", "Mara held very still.", "Peregrine's radar and inertial estimates continued. The last metres took much longer than metres normally should.", "Contact.", "A slight movement in the camera. Engine shutdown.", "For three seconds no one spoke from the surface.", "Then Nia's voice arrived.", "“Peregrine is down. Stable attitude. All crew well.”", "Ivo shut his eyes.", "Saira gripped the edge of the table.", "Lin acknowledged the landing in a voice that belonged to the official record.", "Mara looked at the beacon trace.", "It had continued transmitting throughout.", "A modest rectangle on a ridge, doing one useful thing."]}, {"chapter": 9, "chapterTitle": "The Part Where Nothing Happens", "paragraphs": ["The first celebration consisted of four people checking that nothing still required their immediate attention.", "Then Ivo clapped once, much too loudly in the small room.", "Saira laughed. Lin sat down as though somebody had removed a support from behind her knees.", "Mara found she was shaking.", "“Normal,” Saira said, passing her food.", "“I'm not hungry.”", "“Also normal. Eat it.”", "The tug's crew began post-landing checks. South Haven dispatched the unloading rover. The pump would not install itself, and the medical crates had a temperature schedule to keep.", "No brass band. No planetary declaration.", "Tomas sent a message containing only the words QUIET PUMP ACQUIRED.", "Mara laughed harder than the words deserved.", "Nia called when the spacecraft was secure.", "“Your beacon behaved.”", "“We've been speaking firmly to it.”", "“Keep the logs. Our engineers want them.”", "“Already copying.”", "A pause, long enough to become personal.", "“Thank you,” Nia said.", "Mara had prepared several professional responses. None survived the moment.", "“You're welcome.”", "After the call, Lin authorised the temporary beacon to remain in monitored service until the new oscillator could arrive from South Haven.", "Then she sent half the crew to bed.", "This time, nobody argued."]}, {"chapter": 9, "chapterTitle": "The Part Where Nothing Happens", "paragraphs": ["Mara woke eight hours later to silence from the navigation alarms and a new fault notification.", "GALLEY WATER HEATER: REPAIR IN PROGRESS.", "She found Ivo on the floor beside an open panel, surrounded by tools arranged in a semicircle that discouraged casual conversation.", "“Thought you were fixing the fan.”", "“Negotiations developed.”", "The kettle's heating element was fine. Its protective cutoff had opened because a ventilation grille behind the cupboard had become blocked with packaging foam.", "Mara looked at the foam.", "It came from the new timing rack's installation kit.", "For a moment neither spoke.", "Then Ivo began to laugh.", "Not because the connection was clever. Because the universe had displayed a level of petty consistency that deserved recognition.", "“The same refit,” Mara said.", "“Different failure mode.”", "“Common cause.”", "“Tea-related consequence.”", "They cleared the grille, inspected the wiring and tested the cutoff before restoring power.", "The kettle heated.", "Mara stood with her mug while the first steam rose inside its covered dispensing chamber.", "Beyond the window the Moon remained indifferent, inhospitable and spectacular.", "She had never loved the view more."]}, {"chapter": 10, "chapterTitle": "The Things We Leave Working", "paragraphs": ["The incident review took three days.", "It would have been quicker if they had selected one person to blame and stopped asking questions. Lin did not permit that shortcut.", "They mapped the cracked retaining clip, the untested recovery path, the missing bulletin, the retired repeater and the refit's assumption of independence. They included Mara's approval, Ivo's unconverted objection and management's incomplete configuration records.", "They also recorded the decisions that had worked: suspending the service early, respecting rover reserves, abandoning the clamp, using independent reviewers, publishing uncertainty and preserving the tug commander's authority.", "“People need to know what to repeat,” Lin said. “As well as what to stop.”", "Rusk joined from Earth with the first network-wide audit results. Two other stations contained the same board revision. Neither showed the fault, but both would be isolated before their next navigation window.", "A third had an undocumented heritage receiver.", "“Only three?” Ivo asked.", "“So far,” Rusk said.", "They all appreciated the accuracy.", "Mara wrote a separate note about fatigue and the nearly abandoned suit warning.", "It was the hardest part to make public.", "It was also the part she most wanted some future tired engineer to read."]}, {"chapter": 10, "chapterTitle": "The Things We Leave Working", "paragraphs": ["The new oscillator arrived in a small cargo case with an extravagant quantity of protective foam.", "Ivo stood over it as Mara unpacked.", "“Not a piece behind a cupboard.”", "“I know.”", "“I'm supervising history.”", "They installed the unit, tested its reference chain independently and deliberately interrupted every input the design claimed to survive. The recovery mode no longer accepted heritage transmitters. Its status display now distinguished accurate, holding, uncertain and unavailable.", "No single green light.", "Mara liked that immensely.", "Lantern received its own work order. A survey rover would isolate its transmitter and verify the isolation locally. Decommissioned would become a measured state, not a hopeful word in a database.", "Orison would remain protected as a historic scientific site. Its accessible measurements would be copied through a new low-power receiver on a future visit.", "Mara asked whether the improvised clamp could be collected.", "The heritage officer replied that it now formed part of the site's documented history and should remain until a controlled recovery was approved.", "Ivo read the message over her shoulder.", "“Your adaptor's in a museum.”", "“Temporarily.”", "“That's what every museum object thinks.”", "She saved the reply anyway."]}, {"chapter": 10, "chapterTitle": "The Things We Leave Working", "paragraphs": ["A week after the landing, South Haven invited the Kestrel crew to a remote dinner.", "Travelling between stations was still too expensive to waste on ceremony, so they put one screen at the end of each table and pretended the communication delay was merely good manners.", "Tomas showed them the new pump.", "It made a steady, unobtrusive sound.", "Ivo listened with his head tilted.", "“Beautiful.”", "“We thought you'd like it.”", "Nia's crew joined from Peregrine before departure. Someone had printed a sign for the tug's galley: DECLARED UNCERTAINTY IS A FORM OF AFFECTION.", "Mara groaned.", "Saira asked for a copy.", "The conversation wandered from the landing to food, families and a rumour that the next habitat shipment contained fresh onions. The onions occupied more discussion than the navigation incident.", "Mara found this reassuring.", "Machines mattered because people wanted to keep living ordinary lives around them. A repaired pump meant showers, clean surfaces, plants growing under lamps. A safe landing meant a person could complain about dinner and expect another dinner tomorrow.", "At the end, Tomas raised a covered cup.", "“To the relay station.”", "Ivo raised his.", "“And to preventive maintenance.”", "There was a respectful silence.", "Then someone asked if that came with onions."]}, {"chapter": 10, "chapterTitle": "The Things We Leave Working", "paragraphs": ["Mara called her father on her next rest day.", "He had read the public incident summary, including the line about a previously unknown interaction between legacy recovery systems.", "“Previously unknown to whom?” he asked.", "“To everyone involved. Apparently.”", "“Useful distinction.”", "She showed him the photograph of the emergency antenna.", "He studied it with the grave attention he once gave badly mounted marine aerials.", "“Cable run looks tidy.”", "“Saira and Lin did it.”", "“Tell them.”", "“I have.”", "“Tell them again. People hear about untidy work plenty.”", "Rain moved across his kitchen window. Somewhere off-screen a delivery driver knocked, and he went to answer the door. Mara waited, listening to the ordinary sounds of a place where you could open a door without a checklist.", "When he returned he held a small parcel.", "“What is it?”", "“New kettle.”", "“Your old one's broken?”", "“No. Your sister sent it. Says I should have a spare.”", "Mara laughed.", "He smiled at the screen.", "“You sound better.”", "“I've slept.”", "“Useful technology.”", "After the call she opened the notebook and wrote beneath his old advice: Independence must be demonstrated. Also, listen when people say they are tired.", "She left room underneath.", "She expected to need it."]}, {"chapter": 10, "chapterTitle": "The Things We Leave Working", "paragraphs": ["At 06:12 the next morning, Mara made tea.", "The kettle worked. The galley fan hummed evenly. Outside, a maintenance rover was inspecting the clips along the new cable route before the temporary system was finally removed.", "Kit announced an incoming packet on the heritage monitoring channel.", "Ivo lowered his mug.", "“If that's another forty-three-year-old fault, I'm on annual leave.”", "Mara opened the display.", "Orison's emergency summary had arrived again. Its small transmitter was still repeating the report, exactly as designed. Nobody had yet visited to clear the queue.", "This time Kestrel recognised the source without trusting it as a clock. It saved the packet as scientific data and left the navigation system alone.", "The systems disagreed safely.", "Mara logged the reception and added it to the planned follow-up. No urgency flag. No grand announcement.", "Just a job, understood at last.", "Beyond the window the antenna dishes tracked spacecraft she could not see. The settlement lights glimmered beyond a distant ridge. Somewhere, a pump moved water. Somewhere, people were waking, making plans, losing teaspoons.", "The future had turned out to be full of maintenance.", "Mara took her first sip.", "Fortunately, it was also full of people who knew where the tools were.", "THE END"]}];
